1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,800
每当有人说容器共享操作系统时，接下来的问题总是同一个。
Every time someone says containers share the operating system, the next question is the same.

2
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:06,400
那它们是怎么分离的呢？
Then how are they separate?

3
00:00:06,862 --> 00:00:11,236
这个答案就在 Linux 的两个概念里：namespaces 和 cgroups。
That answer lives in two Linux ideas, namespaces and C groups.

4
00:00:11,694 --> 00:00:13,280
听起来挺吓人的，但其实没那么复杂。
They sound scary, they are not.

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:17,572
一个决定进程能看到什么，另一个决定它能用多少资源。
One decides what a process can see, the other decides how much it can use.

6
00:00:18,094 --> 00:00:18,564
就是这么简单。
That is it.

7
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,700
看到和使用，隐私和限制。
Seeing and using, privacy and limits.

8
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:23,972
让我们从 namespaces 开始。
Let us start with namespaces.

9
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:26,596
想象你和朋友住在同一栋房子里。
Imagine you and a friend are in the same house.

10
00:00:26,926 --> 00:00:32,640
如果没有 namespaces，你们都能看到每个房间、每个抽屉、每个进程，甚至每一根网线。
Without namespaces, you both see every room, every drawer, every process, every network cable.

11
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:33,440
那简直是一片混乱。
Chaos.

12
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:37,732
namespace 给你的进程提供了一个关于系统部分的私人视角。
A namespace gives your process its own private view of part of the system.

13
00:00:38,126 --> 00:00:39,812
同样的房子，不同的窗户。
Same house, different windows.

14
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:42,800
Linux 有好几种重要的 namespaces。
Linux has several important namespaces.

15
00:00:43,054 --> 00:00:44,388
每一个都隐藏不同的东西。
Each one hides a different thing.

16
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,852
首先是进程 namespace。
First, the process namespace.

17
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,332
通常情况下，每个程序都可以通过像 PS 这样的命令看到所有其他程序。
Normally every program can see every other program with a command like PS.

18
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:55,652
在进程 namespace 内部，你的容器只能看到它自己的进程。
Inside a process namespace, your container only sees its own processes.

19
00:00:56,046 --> 00:00:58,052
对它来说，它看起来就像是编号为 1 的进程。
To it, it looks like process number one.

20
00:00:58,478 --> 00:00:59,972
机器上的其他内容都消失了。
The rest of the machine disappears.

21
00:01:00,654 --> 00:01:05,988
这就是为什么容器内的程序觉得自己是独占的，即使旁边还有 50 个容器在运行。
That is why a program inside a container thinks it is alone, even when 50 other containers are running next door.

22
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:08,260
第二是挂载 namespace。
Second, the mount namespace.

23
00:01:08,558 --> 00:01:10,212
它控制着文件系统的视图。
This controls the file system view.

24
00:01:10,574 --> 00:01:13,680
一个容器可以看到根目录，并认为那就是整个磁盘。
One container can see slash and think that is the whole disk.

25
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:16,484
另一个容器则可能拥有完全不同的文件夹集合。
Another container can have a completely different set of folders.

26
00:01:17,038 --> 00:01:19,812
除非你故意共享，否则你的宿主机文件都会保持隐藏。
Your host files stay hidden unless you deliberately share them.

27
00:01:20,238 --> 00:01:22,240
这比老式的 Chroot 要高级得多。
This is a big upgrade from old school Charot.

28
00:01:22,606 --> 00:01:25,490
Chroot 可以更改根目录，但更容易逃逸。
Charoot could change the root folder, but it was easier to escape.

29
00:01:25,966 --> 00:01:28,004
挂载 namespace 更强大且更干净。
Mount namespaces are stronger and cleaner.

30
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:30,276
第三是网络 namespace。
Third, the network namespace.

31
00:01:30,702 --> 00:01:31,364
这个很有意思。
This one is fun.

32
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:38,160
每个网络 namespace 都可以拥有自己的网络接口、自己的 IP 地址、自己的路由表和自己的端口。
Each network namespace can have its own network interfaces, its own IP address, its own routing table, and its own ports.

33
00:01:38,574 --> 00:01:42,212
所以两个容器可以在各自的世界里同时监听 80 端口。
So two containers can both listen on port 80 inside their own world.

34
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:43,044
不会冲突。
No fight.

35
00:01:43,502 --> 00:01:46,244
从外部看，如果需要的话，你可以把它们映射到不同的主机端口。
From outside, you map those to different host ports if you need to.

36
00:01:46,958 --> 00:01:48,560
第四个是用户 namespace。
Fourth, the user namespace.

37
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:49,796
这是关于身份的。
This is about identity.

38
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:52,800
在容器内部，一个进程可能认为自己是 root。
Inside the container, a process can think it is root.

39
00:01:53,038 --> 00:01:56,772
但在外部，Linux 可以把这个映射到主机上的一个普通、无权限的用户。
Outside, Linux can map that to a normal, unprivileged user on the host.

40
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,204
这是一个非常强大的安全技巧。
That is a powerful safety trick.

41
00:01:59,598 --> 00:02:02,884
容器内的 root 并不意味着你整台机器的 root。
Root inside does not automatically mean root of your whole machine.

42
00:02:03,918 --> 00:02:06,116
第五个是 UTS namespace。
Fifth, the UTS namespace.

43
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:07,920
虽然它很小，但很有用。
A small one, but useful.

44
00:02:08,238 --> 00:02:10,400
它让容器可以拥有自己的主机名。
It lets a container have its own host name.

45
00:02:10,734 --> 00:02:17,220
所以你的 Web 容器可以叫 Web1，数据库容器可以叫 DB1，而不需要更改你电脑的实际名称。
So your web container can be called Web1, and your database container can be called DB1, without renaming your actual computer.

46
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,240
第六个是 IPC namespace。
Six, the IPC namespace.

47
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:24,160
它隔离了诸如共享内存之类的进程间通信内容。
This isolates inter-process communication stuff like shared memory.

48
00:02:24,494 --> 00:02:27,108
通过这些通道进行通信的程序会保持在各自的轨道上。
Programs that talk through those channels stay in their own lane.

49
00:02:27,694 --> 00:02:33,860
还有更多细节和更新的 namespace 类型，但这些已经解释了人们所说的容器隔离的大部分内容。
There are more details and newer namespace types, but these already explain most of what people mean by container isolation.

50
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:37,124
所以 namespaces 回答了一个问题：
So namespaces answer one question.

51
00:02:37,518 --> 00:02:39,076
“我被允许看到什么？”
What am I allowed to see?

52
00:02:39,694 --> 00:02:40,480
现在看第二部分。
Now the second half.

53
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:41,444
C groups。
C groups.

54
00:02:42,734 --> 00:02:44,640
C groups 代表控制组。
C groups stands for control groups.

55
00:02:45,134 --> 00:02:49,124
如果说 namespaces 是关于隐私，那么 C groups 就是关于公平和限制。
If namespaces are about privacy, C groups are about fairness and limits.

56
00:02:49,838 --> 00:02:55,908
如果没有 C groups，一个贪婪的程序可能会吃掉所有
的 CPU，占满所有的内存，让其他所有东西都变慢。
Without C groups, one greedy program can eat all the CPU, fill all the memory, and make everything else crawl.

57
00:02:56,654 --> 00:03:00,196
有了 C groups，Linux 就可以把进程放入不同的组并设定规则。
With C groups, Linux can put processes into groups and set rules.

58
00:03:00,718 --> 00:03:04,420
你可以规定，这个组最多只能使用两个 CPU 核心。
You can say, this group gets at most two CPU cores.

59
00:03:04,814 --> 00:03:07,716
这个组只能使用 512MB 的内存。
This group can use only 512 megabytes of memory.

60
00:03:08,334 --> 00:03:10,880
这个组每秒只能向磁盘写入这么多数据。
This group can write only so much to disk per second.

61
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:13,764
这个组只能使用这么多网络带宽。
This group can use only this much network bandwidth.

62
00:03:14,990 --> 00:03:18,532
如果进程试图超出内存限制，Linux 可以阻止它。
If the process tries to go beyond the memory limit, Linux can stop it.

63
00:03:18,894 --> 00:03:20,292
这样就能保护机器上的其他部分。
That protects the rest of the machine.

64
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,240
这就是为什么容器在真实服务器上非常实用。
This is why containers feel practical on real servers.

65
00:03:24,558 --> 00:03:28,612
你不仅是在隐藏东西，还在防止「吵闹的邻居」破坏派对。
You are not just hiding things, you are also preventing noisy neighbors from ruining the party.

66
00:03:29,518 --> 00:03:30,564
让我们把这个具体化。
Let us make this concrete.

67
00:03:31,246 --> 00:03:33,520
假设你在一台笔记本电脑上运行三个容器。
Suppose you run three containers on one laptop.

68
00:03:33,902 --> 00:03:35,280
容器 A 是你的 Web 应用。
Container A is your web app.

69
00:03:35,758 --> 00:03:37,200
容器 B 是一个数据库。
Container B is a database.

70
00:03:37,518 --> 00:03:40,036
容器 C 是一个压缩视频的批处理任务。
Container C is a batch job that compresses videos.

71
00:03:40,846 --> 00:03:44,548
namespaces 确保 A 默认情况下看不到 B 的进程或文件。
Namespaces make sure A cannot see B's processes or files by default.

72
00:03:45,070 --> 00:03:49,444
cgroups 确保 C 在你测试时不会占用全部 CPU 并导致 Web 应用卡死。
C groups make sure C cannot grab all the CPU and freeze the web app while you are testing.

73
00:03:50,062 --> 00:03:50,720
同样的机器。
Same machine.

74
00:03:51,054 --> 00:03:53,124
独立的视图，受控的资源。
Separate views, controlled resources.

75
00:03:53,614 --> 00:03:56,292
这种组合就是现代容器背后的真正魔力。
That pairing is the real magic behind modern containers.

76
00:03:57,262 --> 00:03:59,012
很多人认为 Docker 发明了这一切。
A lot of people think Docker invented this.

77
00:03:59,280 --> 00:03:59,920
其实并不是。
It did not.

78
00:04:00,238 --> 00:04:01,508
Docker 让它变得好用。
Docker made it usable.

79
00:04:01,774 --> 00:04:03,940
基础技术早已存在于 Linux 中。
The foundations were already in Linux.

80
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:10,240
早在 Docker 出名之前，像 LXC 这样的项目就已经在利用这些内核特性构建容器了。
Years before Docker became famous, projects like LXC were already building containers from these same kernel features.

81
00:04:10,542 --> 00:04:17,988
Docker 的重大胜利在于它封装了工作流，让普通开发者无
需像内核工程师那样去操作，就能构建、交付和运行容器。
Docker's big win was packaging the workflow so everyday developers could build, ship,
and run containers without feeling like kernel engineers.

82
00:04:18,446 --> 00:04:29,828
事实上，如果你把容器简化到核心思想，那就是：启动一个进程，把它放入新的 namespaces，
绑定到 cgroups，给它一个文件系统，可能再给它一个网络。
In fact, if you strip containers down to the core idea, you get this start a process, put it in new namespaces, attach it to C groups,
give it a file system, maybe give it a network.

83
00:04:30,222 --> 00:04:31,520
这就是它的配方。
That is the recipe.

84
00:04:31,982 --> 00:04:36,996
像 Docker、Podman 和 Containerd 这样的工具，只是这些内核特性的友好前端。
Tools like Docker, Podman, and Container D are friendly front ends over those kernel features.

85
00:04:38,862 --> 00:04:40,128
为什么初学者也需要关心这些？
Why should a beginner care?

86
00:04:40,398 --> 00:04:44,928
因为一旦你理解了 namespaces 和 cgroups，容器就不再像是神秘的魔盒了。
Because once you understand namespaces and C groups, containers stop feeling like magic boxes.

87
00:04:45,088 --> 00:04:48,228
它们变成了围绕普通 Linux 进程的一层巧妙伪装。
They become a clever costume around normal Linux processes.

88
00:04:49,774 --> 00:04:51,248
这种思维转变非常有帮助。
And that mental shift helps a lot.

89
00:04:51,488 --> 00:04:55,008
突然间，日志变得更合乎逻辑，网络也变得更合乎逻辑。
Suddenly logs make more sense, networking makes more sense.

90
00:04:55,342 --> 00:04:58,768
为什么一个容器能看到某些文件而看不到其他文件，也变得更合乎逻辑了。
Why a container can see some files and not others makes more sense.

91
00:04:59,086 --> 00:05:02,768
为什么 Kubernetes 在意 CPU 和内存请求，也就好理解了。
Why Kubernetes cares about CPU and memory requests makes more sense.

92
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:06,660
那些 Kubernetes 的数值，本质上就是换了包装的 cgroups 设置。
Those Kubernetes numbers are basically C group settings with better branding.

93
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:14,340
当有人说给这个 pod 分配 250 毫核和 512 兆时，他们是在描述预算。
When someone says, give this pod 250 millicores and 512 megs, they are describing a budget.

94
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:16,740
Linux 通过 cgroups 来强制执行这些预算。
Linux enforces budgets through C groups.

95
00:05:18,702 --> 00:05:21,808
让我们来看看一个进程变成容器的简单过程。
Let us walk through a simple story of a process becoming a container.

96
00:05:22,286 --> 00:05:23,648
一个进程在 Linux 上启动。
A process starts on Linux.

97
00:05:23,918 --> 00:05:25,968
默认情况下，它与主机共享所有内容。
By default, it shares everything with the host.

98
00:05:26,254 --> 00:05:30,372
相同的进程列表、相同的网络、相同的挂载点、相同的资源池。
Same process list, same network, same mounts, same resource pool.

99
00:05:31,278 --> 00:05:33,488
然后运行时环境为它创建新的 namespaces。
Then the runtime creates new namespaces for it.

100
00:05:33,806 --> 00:05:38,564
新的进程视图、新的挂载视图、新的网络视图，可能还有用户映射。
New process view, new mount view, new network view, maybe a user mapping too.

101
00:05:39,342 --> 00:05:42,948
然后它把那个进程放入带有 CPU 和内存限制的 cgroups 中。
Then it places that process into C groups with CPU and memory limits.

102
00:05:45,294 --> 00:05:48,608
接着它设置好文件系统层，让进程看到它自己的路径。
Then it sets up the file system layer so the process sees its own route.

103
00:05:48,974 --> 00:05:50,692
现在这个进程就生活在一个气泡里了。
Now that process is living in a bubble.

104
00:05:51,150 --> 00:05:57,892
它不是虚拟机，也不是完整的客户操作系统，只是一个经
过精心过滤的 Linux 视图，并且限制了它的资源消耗。
Not a virtual machine, not a full guest operating system, just a carefully filtered view of Linux with a leash on its appetite.

105
00:05:58,350 --> 00:05:59,428
这就是容器。
That is a container.

106
00:06:00,142 --> 00:06:02,980
人们经常问，这和虚拟机一样安全吗？
People often ask, is this as safe as a virtual machine?

107
00:06:03,408 --> 00:06:04,528
通常不安全。
Usually no.

108
00:06:04,846 --> 00:06:09,568
虚拟机在更硬的边界上进行隔离，因为每个虚拟机都有自己的内核。
Virtual machines isolate at a harder boundary because each VM has its own kernel.

109
00:06:09,966 --> 00:06:11,524
容器共享主机的内核。
Containers share the host kernel.

110
00:06:11,758 --> 00:06:15,364
如果内核有漏洞，容器之间的墙就会变弱。
If the kernel has a bad bug, the wall between containers is weaker.

111
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:17,728
这就是为什么安全问题依然很重要。
That is why security still matters.

112
00:06:18,126 --> 00:06:19,328
保持镜像更新。
Keep images updated.

113
00:06:19,598 --> 00:06:21,568
除非必须，否则不要以 root 身份运行。
Do not run as root unless you must.

114
00:06:21,870 --> 00:06:23,488
丢弃不必要的权限。
Drop unnecessary permissions.

115
00:06:23,886 --> 00:06:25,968
能用用户 namespaces 的时候就用。
Use user namespaces when you can.

116
00:06:26,318 --> 00:06:27,328
限制资源。
Limit resources.

117
00:06:27,598 --> 00:06:30,528
只共享你打算共享的文件和设备。
Share only the files and devices you intend to share.

118
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:34,368
namespaces 和 cgroups 为你提供了强大的实际隔离。
Namespaces and C groups give you strong practical isolation.

119
00:06:34,528 --> 00:06:36,452
它们并不是完美的安全咒语。
They are not a spell of perfect safety.

120
00:06:37,198 --> 00:06:39,488
当你深入研究时，还会听到另一个关键要素。
There is one more piece you'll hear about as you go deeper.

121
00:06:39,886 --> 00:06:41,796
Linux capabilities 和 seccomp。
Linux capabilities and seccomp.

122
00:06:42,414 --> 00:06:46,692
Capabilities 把旧的、全能的 root 模型拆分成了更小的权限。
Capabilities break the old, all powerful root model into smaller permissions.

123
00:06:47,246 --> 00:06:48,356
需要绑定低端口吗？
Need to bind a low port?

124
00:06:48,718 --> 00:06:52,208
也许你只需要其中一种能力，而不是宇宙中所有的超能力。
Maybe you need one capability, not every superpower in the universe.

125
00:06:52,686 --> 00:06:56,208
Seccomp 可以过滤进程允许执行哪些系统调用。
Setcomp can filter which system calls a process is allowed to make.

126
00:06:56,526 --> 00:06:57,728
减少危险的入口。
Fewer dangerous doors.

127
00:06:58,062 --> 00:06:59,236
缩小攻击面。
Smaller attack surface.

128
00:06:59,918 --> 00:07:02,368
第一天你不需要这些，只要知道这一点就行。
You do not need those on day one, just know this.

129
00:07:02,766 --> 00:07:05,328
namespaces 和 cgroups 是主舞台。
Namespaces and C groups are the main stage.

130
00:07:05,614 --> 00:07:08,580
Capabilities 和 seccomp 是门上的额外锁。
Capabilities and setcomp are extra locks on the doors.

131
00:07:09,326 --> 00:07:10,768
另一个常见的误区。
Another common confusion.

132
00:07:11,086 --> 00:07:13,348
namespaces 只是为了容器吗？
Are namespaces only for containers?

133
00:07:13,808 --> 00:07:14,128
不是。
No.

134
00:07:14,606 --> 00:07:16,368
Linux 可以用它们来做很多事情。
Linux can use them for lots of things.

135
00:07:16,718 --> 00:07:21,568
有些 VPN 工具使用网络 namespaces，有些测试工具用来隔离进程。
Some VPN tools use network namespaces, some testing tools isolate processes.

136
00:07:21,934 --> 00:07:27,556
Systemd 大量使用 cgroups 来管理你机器上的服务，甚至在完全不涉及 Docker 的时候也是如此。
System D uses C groups heavily to manage services on your machine, even when Docker is not involved at all.

137
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:34,128
如果你使用现代的 Linux 桌面或服务器，cgroups 已经在默默地限制服务能消耗多少资源了。
If you use a modern Linux desktop or server, C groups are already quietly shaping what services can consume.

138
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:36,356
所以这不是冷门知识。
So this is not niche knowledge.

139
00:07:36,558 --> 00:07:38,404
这就是 Linux 管理工作的方式。
This is how Linux manages work.

140
00:07:39,278 --> 00:07:41,808
让我们再把这两个概念放在一起对比一下。
Let us put the two ideas side by side one more time.

141
00:07:42,222 --> 00:07:45,316
namespaces 说：这是你对系统的私人视角。
Namespaces say, here is your private view of the system.

142
00:07:45,934 --> 00:07:47,748
cgroups 说：这是你的预算。
C groups say, here is your budget.

143
00:07:48,334 --> 00:07:51,808
隐私加上预算，就等于一个可控的微型环境。
Privacy plus budget equals a controllable mini environment.

144
00:07:52,302 --> 00:07:54,528
这就是为什么云平台喜欢容器。
That is why cloud platforms love containers.

145
00:07:54,688 --> 00:07:59,568
它们可以在一台机器上打包很多工作负载，而不需要每个工作负载都运行完整的虚拟机。
They can pack many workloads onto one machine without every workload needing a full virtual machine.

146
00:07:59,886 --> 00:08:02,948
更高的密度、更快的启动、更低的开销。
Higher density, faster startup, lower overhead.

147
00:08:03,534 --> 00:08:06,048
虚拟机启动的是整个操作系统。
A virtual machine boots a whole operating system.

148
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:10,288
容器是在准备好的 namespaces 和 cgroups 中启动一个进程。
A container starts a process into prepared namespaces and C groups.

149
00:08:10,448 --> 00:08:12,708
这是在速度和重量上的巨大差异。
That is a huge difference in speed and weight.

150
00:08:13,326 --> 00:08:17,348
但要记住权衡：共享内核意味着在最深层是共享命运。
But remember the trade off shared kernel means shared fate at the deepest layer.

151
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:20,420
现在，我给几个方便记忆的例子。
Now, a few practical pictures you can remember.

152
00:08:21,070 --> 00:08:24,356
把 namespaces 想象成同一栋教学楼里的不同教室。
Think of namespaces like separate classrooms in the same school building.

153
00:08:24,608 --> 00:08:30,180
每个教室都有自己的黑板、自己的考勤表、自己的门，但教学楼的地基是共用的。
Each class has its own board, its own attendance list, its own door, but the building foundation is shared.

154
00:08:30,862 --> 00:08:33,248
把 cgroups 想象成每个教室的校规。
Think of C groups like school rules for each class.

155
00:08:33,518 --> 00:08:35,844
这个班可以在电脑室待一小时。
This class gets one hour in the computer lab.

156
00:08:36,110 --> 00:08:38,048
那个班只能借用两个投影仪。
That class can borrow only two projectors.

157
00:08:38,350 --> 00:08:40,515
没有任何一个班可以霸占整个食堂。
No class gets to hog the entire cafeteria.

158
00:08:40,846 --> 00:08:43,940
容器就是拥有私人房间和明确预算的教室。
Containers are classes with private rooms and clear budgets.

159
00:08:44,368 --> 00:08:47,588
一旦理解了这一点，很多关于 Docker 的术语就变简单了。
Once that clicks, a lot of Docker talk becomes plain English.

160
00:08:48,014 --> 00:08:52,516
当 Docker 说端口映射时，那是网络 namespace 与主机网络的对接。
When Docker says port mapping, that is network namespace stuff meeting the host network.

161
00:08:52,688 --> 00:08:56,768
当 Docker 说卷挂载时，那是跨挂载视图的谨慎共享。
When Docker says volume mount, that is carefully sharing a folder across mount views.

162
00:08:57,008 --> 00:08:59,524
当 Docker 说内存限制时，那就是 cgroup。
When Docker says memory limit, that is a C group.

163
00:08:59,982 --> 00:09:03,168
当容器有自己的主机名时，那就是 UTS namespace。
When a container has its own host name, that is a UTS namespace.

164
00:09:03,328 --> 00:09:07,908
当容器里的 root 不是主机上的真实 root 时，那就是用户 namespace 映射。
When root in a container is not real root on the host, that is user namespace mapping.

165
00:09:08,398 --> 00:09:09,508
你看，都是同样的原理。
See, same ideas.

166
00:09:09,838 --> 00:09:10,820
只是更友好的标签而已。
Friendlier labels.

167
00:09:11,662 --> 00:09:13,700
在结束之前，我们快速辟几个谣。
Here is a quick myth check before we wrap up.

168
00:09:14,158 --> 00:09:16,928
谣言一：容器是微型虚拟机。
Myth one, containers are tiny virtual machines.

169
00:09:17,166 --> 00:09:19,940
不是，它们是共享内核的隔离进程。
No, they are isolated processes with shared kernels.

170
00:09:20,494 --> 00:09:23,328
谣言二：如果不是容器，就是完全安全的。
Myth two, if it isn't a container, it is fully safe.

171
00:09:23,662 --> 00:09:26,628
不是，配置和内核安全依然很重要。
No, configuration and kernel security still matter.

172
00:09:26,928 --> 00:09:29,476
谣言三：Docker 就是隔离技术。
Myth three, Docker is the isolation.

173
00:09:29,968 --> 00:09:30,288
不是。
No.

174
00:09:30,766 --> 00:09:33,168
Linux namespaces 和 cgroups 才是隔离技术。
Linux namespaces and C groups are the isolation.

175
00:09:33,486 --> 00:09:34,564
Docker 是工作流。
Docker is the workflow.

176
00:09:35,182 --> 00:09:38,448
谣言四：只有在大型公司工作才需要这些。
Myth four, you only need this if you work at a huge company.

177
00:09:38,798 --> 00:09:43,652
不是，即使是个人项目，如果每个应用都有自己的环境和限制，也会变得更整洁。
No, even a personal project gets cleaner when every app carries its own environment and limits.

178
00:09:44,526 --> 00:09:46,916
如果你想用一句话记住，就记这个。
If you want one sentence for your brain, use this.

179
00:09:47,438 --> 00:09:49,764
Namespaces 负责隐藏，cgroups 负责限制。
Namespaces hide, c groups limit.

180
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:51,428
容器将两者结合在了一起。
Containers combine both.

181
00:09:51,982 --> 00:09:55,008
如果你想知道这对你的职业生涯有什么意义，就看这一句。
And if you want one reason this matters for your career, use this.

182
00:09:55,470 --> 00:10:02,788
云原生计算是建立在这样一个理念上的：应用程序应
该被打包、隔离、限制、快速启动，并且易于迁移。
Cloud native computing is built on the idea that applications should
be packaged, isolated, limited, started quickly, and moved easily.

183
00:10:03,248 --> 00:10:06,980
namespaces 和 cgroups 就是支撑这一理念的 Linux 引擎。
Namespaces and C groups are the Linux engine under that idea.

184
00:10:07,662 --> 00:10:11,008
你不需要在第一天就记住每个子系统里的每一个参数。
You do not need to memorize every flag in every subsystem on day one.

185
00:10:11,214 --> 00:10:12,292
你只需要掌握地图。
You just need the map.

186
00:10:12,942 --> 00:10:14,468
这个进程能看到什么？
What can this process see?

187
00:10:14,926 --> 00:10:16,900
这个进程被允许消耗多少资源？
How much is this process allowed to consume?

188
00:10:17,486 --> 00:10:20,260
Linux 通过 namespaces 和 cgroups 来回答这些问题。
Linux answers those with namespaces and C groups.

189
00:10:20,942 --> 00:10:24,288
一旦你理解了这一点，容器终于不再神秘了。
And once you see that, containers finally stop feeling mysterious.

190
00:10:24,526 --> 00:10:25,540
它们变得理所当然。
They feel inevitable.

191
00:10:26,286 --> 00:10:29,060
如果这些内容对你有帮助，请继续关注 BitByteTalks。
If this helped, stick around on BitByteTalks.

192
00:10:29,262 --> 00:10:32,644
我们将那些可怕的 Linux 术语转化成通俗易懂的对话。
We take the scary Linux words and turn them into plain conversation.

193
00:10:33,134 --> 00:10:38,020
下次有人说容器共享内核但依然是隔离的，你就会清楚地知道是怎么实现的。
Next time someone says containers share the kernel but are still isolated, you will know exactly how.

